<!-- GREENHOUSE GASES -->
<section id="ghg">
    <h2>What Are Greenhouse Gases?</h2>
    <div class="flex-row">
        <div class="flex-col">
            <p>
                Greenhouse gases (GHGs) are gases in the atmosphere that trap heat. They allow short‑wave radiation 
                (sunlight) to pass through the atmosphere but absorb long‑wave infrared radiation that the Earth 
                re‑emits. This keeps the planet warm enough for life.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Main greenhouse gases (GCSE):</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Carbon dioxide (CO₂):</strong> Released from burning fossil fuels and deforestation.</li>
                <li><strong>Methane (CH₄):</strong> Produced by cattle, rice fields, and landfill sites.</li>
                <li><strong>Nitrous oxide (N₂O):</strong> Released from fertilisers and industry.</li>
                <li><strong>Water vapour (H₂O):</strong> Naturally present; increases as temperatures rise.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="flex-col">
